designs.summary:
Answer to the owner: yes, today's good meshes are made case by case at the template level. A person (an agent) wrote each object's part list and some windows sized in mm (pot = lathe + flat bar, lid = dome + strap, rack = tray + cup + pegs, boards = slab - slot). Once a template is chosen, everything else is fitted automatically and checked GT-free. This design replaces the per-object templates with one program that has no object names in it:
(1) a QC gate on each episode's track;
(2) marker balls removed first with one shared detector;
(3) over-complete part proposals: lathe axis search, region growing / RANSAC planes, slippage signatures;
(4) a fixed library of about 10 generic parts, every bound relative to fitted size;
(5) residual peeling with a beam search over assemblies;
(6) declared priors for never-seen geometry, never chosen by fidelity;
(7) GT-free selection by z-buffered depth-render agreement on held-out frames, plus untrimmed recall, an MDL-style penalty and the kit-registration flip gate, with library_v3 always a candidate;
(8) a manifold3d union in the library frame, so the eval meshes need no eval depth.

New measurements here [M], $0 on CPU, no scans read.
- I ran every existing fitter on every object: 26 new runs, 48 candidates over 8 objects. 23 runs exited 0 with watertight meshes whatever the object; 3 crashed.
- Selection is the weak link. Five GT-free scores each mis-rank 1 or 2 of the 8 objects, and each fails in a different place. I compared them against the reference orders: existing [D] for cup, pot, lid, rack and board 1; [E] 'library better than a visibly wrong template' for the sweeper, pitcher and dust pan.
  - Standard obs_fidelity picks a flat 8 mm board plate for the sweeper (3.27 vs library 4.22 mm). On ep26 it also prefers library_v3 to the hand rack fit (8.57 vs 9.32), although [D] says 0.555 -> 0.456.
  - A back-face-culled version prefers library_v3 to the cup lathe (3.64 vs 3.75), against [D] 0.523 -> 0.291.
  - Untrimmed recall at 3 mm gets 7/8. It fails on the dust pan, where the lid template's phantom strap raises recall (0.716 vs 0.692).
  - Capped mean gets 7/8 and fails on the rack.
- Thickness that we never see cannot be selected from points. Standard fidelity prefers thin lids (1.436 / 1.686 / 2.259 mm at t = 1.5 / 2.5 / 4.0) because a thin double sheet absorbs noise on both sides: at t 1.5 the nearest sample of 38 % of points is a back face. The culled score is flat (1.654 / 1.647 / 1.715).

Expected result if it works [E]: it reproduces the hand fits on pot, lid and rack, adds handles to the cups, and covers the dust pan and sweeper. CD-O would go from about 0.402 to about 0.33 cm if the hidden scans match the public ones. That is still well above the leaders' 0.11-0.15: this route mostly automates and widens coverage, it does not close the gap.

pipeline_steps:
S0 Inputs (GT-free). Per (public episode, object):
- obs npz from obs_points.py on our own tracks (pts, cam, frame, 4 mm hits/free grid);
- our tracked poses plus calib.json for rendering;
- ego_cam_a video where present (ep2, 11, 22, 23, 26, 31, 39, 41);
- library_v3 as fallback candidate and ROI.
The object name is only a file key; no branch in the code reads it. Output stays in the library frame, as in the 27 Oct kit-of-parts round, so eval meshes are built once from public episodes.

S3 Markers first. Merge the two existing detectors (prim_potlid set A, prim_boards set B) into one:
- centre votes p - R0 n with R0 = 7 mm (from our own spoon stereo), then mean shift and sphere LSQ;
- support from >= 10 frames and a curvature check;
- a stem only where points support it, tested against a 20 mm control offset.
Accepted balls become parts and their points are removed. The balls are physically identical on all objects, so this step needs no object knowledge. It fixes the [M] false ball found on a flat dust-pan patch.

S4 Over-complete proposals, in the recover-and-select style of Leonardis 1995 [L]:
(a) body-of-revolution axes from pot_axis (600 directions, rim flatness + rotational consistency) and lathe_obs.fit_axis candidates;
(b) planar and quadric seeds from region growing on normals plus Schnabel-style RANSAC (probe_ransac.py; [M] alone it explains only 24-60 % of points in 8-83 fragments, so it only seeds);
(c) a slippage signature per large segment (Gelfand-Guibas 6x6 eigenproblem [L]: rotational -> lathe/sheet of revolution, translational -> extrusion/tube, none -> free-form). It only orders the search, never decides;
(d) reflection-plane voting (Mitra 2006 [L]) to tie left/right parameters on bilateral bodies.

S5 Generic part library. Each part has a fit-from-points routine, a parameter count k, relative bounds and a bound-hit flag:
- P1 thin-wall closed-profile lathe: pot_body generalised, arclength profile instead of the polar origin at 60 % height, both orientations tried, wall t from two-wall bins;
- P2 sheet of revolution: build_lid's edge-circle fit and tilt LSQ, dome fill from occluded radial bins;
- P3 planar slab / extruded outline in the part's own PCA plane: prim_boards fit_plane, occupancy, clean_mask, mask_polygon, build_mesh; two-face t when both faces are seen; also the generic handle-plate model, so pot-handle holes come from occupancy;
- P4 folded multi-plate shell (new): plates from P3 joined along fitted intersection lines, offset +-t/2;
- P5 drafted rounded-rect frustum/tube shell: prim_rack rrect_sd and cup_lsq without the hard-coded -y/+x face signs, region seeded from a residual cluster;
- P6 swept tube/strap: the arch_strap sweep along a PCA-plane spline or L1-medial centreline, rect or superellipse section a(s), b(s);
- P7 tapered peg/cone (fit_peg_row) with one template shared by a detected row or lattice (transform voting, Pauly 2008 [L]);
- P8 superquadric / rounded box: EMS-style robust fit, written ourselves because the EMS repo has no clear licence;
- P9 marker ball on stem;
- P10 TSDF patch (tsdf_parts: library_v3 faces outside the fitted parts) as the catch-all.

designs.gt_free_argument:
Inference reads only:
- our FoundationStereo points on our own tracks (obs_points.py);
- our own library_v3 TSDF meshes;
- our colour frames and tracked poses;
- object names, only as file keys.

Scans, *_align.npz, mocap and parquet pose fields are never opened by any step S0-S9. Scans are touched only by mesh_metric.py, after a committed manifest that fixes the selection.

The probes in this run, on the CPU VM, $0, at repo HEAD 704162c (fitter scripts as of e7a411e):
- They read only obs npz keys pts, cam, hits, free, lo, vox, plus our own meshes (library_v3, results/prim, audit outputs).
- I listed a scratchpad directory and saw the name of the scan folder; no scan file was opened.
- The reference orders I compared against quote existing [D] numbers (cup, pot, lid, rack, board 1) or [E] judgements (wrong template on sweeper, pitcher, dust pan). No new scan scoring was done.

Declared risk: choosing the selector after seeing which point scores agree with the existing [D] orders uses public-scan information at the selector level. This is not per object, but it is still information from the scans. Mitigation:
- freeze the selector, including lambda and thresholds, in a commit before the round;
- check it on fresh candidates the search generates;
- never change a selection after scan scoring.

Points cannot settle never-seen geometry, as the [M] lid t-grid shows. It is handled by declared priors that must pass at every grid value, not by fidelity.

pipeline_steps:
2. PART LANGUAGE (written once, object-agnostic). Typed parts, all lengths bounded relative to the fitted object extent rather than absolute mm:
- lathe(axis=pot_axis|fit_axis, wall=measured|grid, floor=closed|open), from prim_potlid pot_axis/pot_body and lathe_obs;
- sor_sheet(t=grid), from the lid's circle and tilt refinement, l.636-684;
- slab(outline=occ|rect, t=measured|grid, holes=seen), from prim_boards fit_plane, clean_mask, mask_polygon, build_mesh;
- plate_set(n, shared_edges, t=grid): new, for folded sheets;
- drafted_shell(rrect|poly, draft, wall=grid, floor=closed|table), from prim_rack rrect_sd, cup_lsq, build_body;
- sweep(centreline=planar|space spline, section=rect|ellipse): the arch_strap sweep loop plus a new centreline fit;
- loft(rings), from prim_rack peg_solid;
- array(part, n, pitch), one shared template;
- marker(r0=7 mm prior, stem=auto);
- patch(source=library_v3|voxel_splat), from tsdf_parts and the lathe_obs splat.
Composition: union and subtract (manifold3d), relations (coaxial, coplanar, attached, symmetric, equal), and point binding (residual_cluster(k), outside(part), inside(part)). The agent may set structure, bindings, discrete options, initial values and relation flags. It may not set free numbers for unseen thicknesses (grid only) or write code.

4. FEEDBACK PANEL (GT-free, training episodes only). Contents:
- obs_fidelity terms per part;
- fs_frac per part and overall, against library_v3;
- residual renders (points more than 4 mm from the mesh);
- mesh overlaid on the crops, with silhouette IoU against the re-seeded masks;
- watertight, body count, every part attached, parameters stuck on bounds;
- rest check: lowest points within 2 mm of the table plane in rest frames, centre of mass over the support;
- flip probe: the kit's 25-start PCA plus trimmed ICP run against OUR fused points instead of the scan, counting distinct optima away from identity;
- parameter count.

designs.gt_free_argument:
**Inference and authoring read only:**
- our FoundationStereo points along our own tracks, the obs npz files including their free/hit vote grids;
- our colour crops, with masks re-seeded from projected observed points instead of from any mesh;
- object names and slot order (allowed);
- the generic part-language documentation;
- library_v3 meshes as a reference baseline (built from public depth, already GT-free).

**Never read by method code, prompts or feedback:** scans (scratchpad loc/t3/mesh/**), *_align.npz, mocap, the parquet 'pose' field, and any repo document. The README and ledger contain scan-derived numbers, e.g. README l.29 wall thicknesses [M].

**Enforcement is structural, not by instruction:** the authoring agent runs with no file-system or web tools. Its bundle is built from an allowlist, every prompt and output is grepped for doc-quoted scan values and forbidden paths, and an auditor re-checks.

**Selection is GT-free, on held-out public episodes.** Library-versus-new is decided only in a separate scan round after programs, meshes and selection are committed. Priors for unseen geometry come from a fixed generic grid that passes at every value; no number is taken from a scan, unlike the first lid and rack round.

**Rules case (c), lit/primitives-cad.md sec. 5.4:** generic parametric parts, every number fitted to our own observations, the name used only as a hint. Built once per object from public episodes and reused for eval: legitimate because the eval episodes use the same physical objects. No public-scan geometry is used as a template.

**Two residual weaknesses to disclose:**
- The fs gate threshold and the decision to keep library-vs-new out of the GT-free selector were informed by knowing which existing meshes passed [D]. This is protocol design from existing diagnostics, not tuning on eval.
- The agent's general world knowledge (what pitchers look like) is a prior, not ground truth.

designs.effort_estimate:
[E] About 6-9 agent work-days in total, spread over several work blocks with saves to STATUS and the work log:
- part-language interpreter, mostly refactoring existing kernels: 2-3 days;
- new parts (sweep centreline fit, plate_set, unified marker detector, loft spout): 1-2 days;
- evidence card, renders, re-seeded crop masks, free-space gate (prototype exists [M]), flip probe on our own points, physical checks: 1 day;
- agent harness (API tool loop, firewall, leak check, logging, freeze): 0.5-1 day;
- Stage A GT-free reproduction test: 0.5 day;
- full run for both arms, held-out obs_points for missing episodes, selection and commit: 1 day;
- scan round plus independent audit: 0.5 day.

The fastest go/no-go comes after about 3-4 days (Stage A), before the new part types are written.

designs.pre_registration_sketch:
Written to docs/work-log.md and committed BEFORE any agent session or scan scoring.

**(1) Code and inputs.**
- Interpreter commit hash, model ID (claude-opus-5-5), effort level, the two prompt texts (hashes), K = 4, at most 8 rounds, stop rule, token budget per session.
- Input allowlist per object; training and held-out episode split per object, fixed in advance:
  - pot: train 41/42, hold-out 43/44/45/2/26/27;
  - rack: train 12/13/27, hold-out 26/31;
  - lid: train 2, hold-out 0;
  - blue cup: train 21, hold-out 22;
  - pitcher: train 22, hold-out 21;
  - dust pan: train 39, hold-out 18/40;
  - sweeper: train 31, hold-out 18;
  - board 1: train 24, hold-out 23.
- Leak-check denylist (the scan values quoted in README l.29 and the ledger, plus forbidden paths).

**(2) Candidate cap.** At most 8 V plus at most 5 R programs per object, each at the prior grid {1.5, 2.5, 4.0} mm (sweeper and other thick-walled classes get a generic grid fixed now, not taken from scans), plus the connected-markers variant.

**(3) Gates.**
- watertight;
- one body after stems;
- fs_frac at most library_v3's per-episode median + 5 pp;
- at most 20 % of parameters on bounds;
- flip probe with 0 off-identity optima over 25 starts;
- rest check for static objects (lowest points within 2 mm of the table, centre of mass inside the support);
- median distance to library_v3 under 5 mm (frame check).

**(4) Selection.** Minimum of held-out sum_mm + 0.01 mm x parameter count, at the middle grid value. library_v3 and the hand meshes are listed but not selectable. Commit the programs, fitted parameters, meshes, transcripts and selection JSON; independent audit of firewall and selection.

**(5) Scan round.** Score each frozen candidate once per grid value with mesh_metric.py (kit CD-O at eval seeds, ADD and flips on at least 6 seeds). The decision uses the selected candidate only; all scores are kept for selector validation (per-object Spearman, reported whatever it shows).

**(6) Pass rule per object:**
- CD-O at most library_v3 − 0.03 cm at EVERY grid value;
- ADD at most library_v3 + 1 mm;
- 0 flips on every seed.
For pot, lid and rack the reference is also reported against the hand-made prim. If V's selected candidate passes against library but is worse than the prim by more than 0.01 cm, keep the prim.

**(7) Decisions and reporting.** Swap only passing objects into the eval pack (pack-only; simulator rebuild per owner decision). Spoon: no pass rule, GT-free report only. Beige cup and board 2 are excluded unless their tracks are fixed and pre-registered separately. Report failures, cost (tokens, $, CPU-h) and measured versus estimated numbers together. Never re-run a session or change a gate after seeing scan scores; any change becomes a new version with a new pre-registration.

designs.summary:
Short answer to the owner: today, yes, the good meshes are partly case by case. An agent picked a template per object, and about a third of the fitter code is knowledge of that one object. This design removes the per-object step. Every object goes through the same three stages, and the only per-object inputs are its name and its public episodes:
(1) a zoo of off-the-shelf proposers, plus our existing fitters, all run on every object;
(2) one generic fit of each proposal to our own stereo points;
(3) one GT-free router that picks per object among all proposals, library_v3 and our family fits.

New measurements here (CPU, $0, no scans read) changed what the zoo should hold:
(a) [M] Whole-object superquadric decomposition, my own re-implementation of the EMS / Solina idea with no third-party code, lost to the best existing mesh on 9/9 objects under the visibility-aware score. It lost to library_v3 on 8/9; the exception is the blue cup, where a solid 2-blob scored 2.37 mm vs lib 2.64 and lathe 1.81. So superquadrics enter the zoo only as part proposals (sweeper block, knobs), and Marching-Primitives and SuperDec drop out.
(b) [M] The current obs_fidelity is unsafe across families. It would pick a superquadric over library_v3 on 3/9 objects:
- the solid blue cup, 4.71 vs 6.07 mm;
- board 2, as a slab with 15x library_v3's area (9,475 vs 616 cm²), 2.51 vs 5.17 mm;
- board 1, 2.36 vs 2.69 mm.
The free-space-aware variant (autoloop_probe/fs_fidelity.py) blocks the two oversized or solid boards, but it ranks the rack joint fit, which passed [D], below library_v3 on ep26 alone (3.61 vs 3.48 mm). So the router must combine free space, silhouettes, an area-sanity gate and hysteresis.
(c) [M] A mesh that arrives in a foreign frame cannot be placed back reliably from our partial points alone. Mean vertex error (ADD) of the chosen pose:
- pot 2.3 mm (4.4 with an 8 % shape error);
- dust pan 5.7–5.8, board 1 5.9–6.7, sweeper 7.3–8.0;
- pitcher 14–18, rack 17–21;
- blue cup 10–49 (handle azimuth lost in 2 of 4 runs);
- lid 115–117 (spun about its axis).
Scale shrank by 6–10 % on the poorly covered objects. In 25/32 runs the wrong pose scored better than the true one, so more search will not fix it.
Consequence: the primary generator is Pixal3D multi-view, which generates in our own camera and object frame (MIT licence). SAM 3D and TRELLIS.2 run second and need silhouette placement plus an ambiguity gate.
(d) [M] Licences checked today. Every learned point-to-CAD model has non-commercial weights:
- cadrille: code Apache-2.0, weights CC-BY-NC-4.0;
- CAD-Recode: CC-BY-NC-4.0;
- MeshCoder: CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0, gated;
- Point2CAD: CC-BY-NC-4.0, outputs surfaces only.
Pixal3D, TRELLIS.2 and SuperDec are MIT.

What to expect [E]: CD-O 0.367 (after the pending prim swap) falls to about 0.29–0.33 cm. Most of the gain comes from the pitcher, beige cup and blue cup. The leaders (0.112–0.154) stay out of reach mainly because of the spoon: 10 pairs with no scan to check against.

pipeline_steps:
S0 Inputs, built once per object from public episodes (GT-free; same object frame as library_v3, so they serve eval with no eval depth):
- obs_points.py npz per episode (pts, cam, frame, hits/free/lo/vox grids);
- crops.py colour crops plus SAM2 masks;
- our own track poses (camera-to-object);
- the object name.
The candidate pool is seeded with library_v3 and with every existing fitter run on every object: lathe_obs, prim_potlid pot and lid, prim_rack, prim_boards. They never refuse ([M] audit), so the router decides.

S2f Rejected proposers:
- Marching-Primitives: MATLAB, and needs a signed SDF we lack in unseen regions;
- SuperDec: solid blobs;
- CAD-Recode and Point2CAD: NC licences; Point2CAD also needs ParSeNet segmentation and outputs surfaces only;
- CoACD: built for collision, not surface accuracy.

S6 Router, GT-free. Score every candidate over all valid episodes of the object with:
- visibility-aware fidelity: obs->mesh plus mesh->obs over observed voxels;
- free-space violation share;
- held-out silhouette IoU;
- area-sanity gate: 0.5–2x library_v3's area;
- registration self-ambiguity (mesh_gates amb_mm);
- a complexity penalty.
The incumbent (prim fit where one passed, else library_v3) is replaced only if the score is at least 10 % better. Write selection.json and commit it before any scan scoring.

designs.what_stays_manual:
Honest list.

Owner decisions:
- permission to run third-party model code (blocked in this sandbox);
- GPU go-ahead and budget;
- requesting SAM 3D gated access and a token that can read gated repos;
- licence calls on non-commercial weights (cadrille, MeshCoder, CAD-Recode) and on Hunyuan's territory exclusion;
- sending the organisers the unsent rules question: does a generated-then-fitted mesh count as 'own reconstruction'?
- approving the pre-registration;
- approving swaps of the packed mesh and the simulator copy.

One-time engineering by an agent (generic, not per object):
- input adapters per generator (Pixal3D's transforms.json convention, SAM 3D pointmap reprojection);
- the GPU environment;
- about 700–900 lines for placement, non-rigid fit, the CAD-literal re-fit and the router.

Judgement still needed:
- visual sanity review of chosen meshes; the eval-view panel caught open bases and see-through holes that no score flagged;
- reading the scan round and declaring pass or fail under the pre-registered rule;
- deciding what to do when the router keeps library_v3.

What automation cannot supply:
- never-seen geometry (undersides, deep cup interiors, the rack floor) is invented by the generator, not measured. No GT-free test can check it, and it is exactly what shifts CD-O's RMS-radius scale.
- the spoon has no scan, so any swap there rests on GT-free evidence alone.
- wrong tracks (board-2 ep23 identity, beige ep11 frame 0) defeat every method; the track-QA gate is a hypothesis.

designs.gt_free_argument:
Inference reads only our own data:
- our FoundationStereo points along our own tracks (obs_points.py);
- our track poses, colour crops and SAM2 masks;
- object names and slot order.
Generators are conditioned on these crops and camera poses only. The fit and the router use only our points, free-space grids and silhouettes. No step reads the scans, *_align.npz, mocap or the parquet pose field.

Pretrained weights are external priors, which the FAQ allows. They were not trained on our scans, as far as can be checked [L]; this cannot be proven.

The router's rules are fixed before any scoring:
- score definition, gates, 10 % hysteresis and seeds are frozen;
- selection.json is committed;
- the scan is read once per candidate, in the pre-registered round.
Unseen-geometry knobs (non-rigid stiffness, CAD literal bounds) are either chosen GT-free or must pass at every value of a pre-declared 3-value grid. Nothing is tuned on scan results.

Eval meshes are built from public episodes and reused in the same frame as library_v3. The organisers' public scans are never used as a template.

Probes run in this task:
- sq_probe.py and sim3_probe.py read only the 'pts' key of the obs npz files;
- fs_fidelity.py also reads that file's own hits/free grids;
- *_align.npz and the scans were not opened.

designs.cost:
CPU, measured [M], 4-core VM, $0:
- whole-object superquadric probe, 9 objects x 2 variants: 4.3 min wall;
- GT-free scoring of 36 meshes: 3 min;
- Sim(3) placement probe, 8 objects x 4 runs: 5.2 min wall, about 75 s per placement per core.
The full CPU stage is about 200 candidates for placement and non-rigid fit: about 1–2 h wall [E]; nricp time is not measured, estimated at 1–3 min per candidate.

GPU, owner go-ahead needed, estimates [E]:
- one A100-80GB or H100 box on Vast at about $0.8–1.7/h (the README's figures; I did not query the price);
- 1–2 h environment setup (TRELLIS.2 base for Pixal3D, SAM 3D);
- runs:
  - Pixal3D-MV, 10 objects x 2 seeds: runtime not published;
  - SAM 3D, about 1 min per frame [L], so 6 frames x 2 seeds is about 15 min per object;
  - TRELLIS.2, 17–60 s per pass on H100 [L];
  - cadrille, under 30 min.
- total about 4–7 h, so about $5–12, ceiling $15.
The box destroys itself on completion, with the stall watchdog per the runbook.

The scan round is CPU, $0.

designs.effort_estimate:
About 2.5–3.5 agent-days before the 2026-11-04 freeze:
- day 1, CPU, $0: promote fs_fidelity into a router with the silhouette, area and ambiguity terms; placement (from sim3_probe.py) with the silhouette term; nricp fit; the CAD-literal re-fit; validate the router on existing candidates (prim, lathe, library_v3, superquadrics);
- day 2, GPU batch after the go-ahead: about 0.5 day including setup, then CPU fits;
- day 3: freeze the selection, run the scan round, write it up, pack.
Owner time: 4–5 decisions.
Slip risk: the GPU environment (flash-attn, natten).

risks.risk:
Licences and permissions block the zoo:
- third-party code is blocked in this sandbox;
- SAM 3D is gated;
- every learned CAD model has non-commercial weights ([M]: cadrille CC-BY-NC-4.0, CAD-Recode CC-BY-NC-4.0, MeshCoder CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0 and gated, Point2CAD CC-BY-NC-4.0);
- Hunyuan excludes EU/UK/KR.

designs.falsification_experiment:
Already run, $0: the whole-object superquadric branch is falsified GT-free. Own re-implementation, 9 objects: it loses to the best existing mesh on 9/9 and to library_v3 on 8/9 under fs_sum.

Next, $0, before any GPU spend: router validation on the existing candidate pool (library_v3, lathe, prim fits, superquadrics, the audit's wrong-template fits) across all public episodes. Freeze the router's choices, then compare them with the already-logged [D] scores, quoting existing scorings only and running no new scan reads. The router is falsified if:
- it fails to pick the passing fit on any of pot, lid, rack, blue-cup lathe; or
- it picks any wrong-template or superquadric mesh over library_v3.

Main test, pre-registered public scan round after the GPU batch. The design is falsified if any of:
- (a) the router's selection meets the pass rule (CD-O <= lib - 0.03 cm, ADD <= lib + 1 mm, 0 flips) on fewer than 3 of the 6 objects without a passing fit today (beige cup, pitcher, dust pan, sweeper, board 1, board 2);
- (b) the median per-object Spearman between router score and CD-O over all candidates is below 0.5;
- (c) any off-the-shelf candidate it selects flips;
- (d) A1 fails: raw Pixal3D-MV obs->mesh is worse than library_v3's on 5 or more of 9 objects, or its rigid-ICP correction to our points exceeds 3 mm on 3 or more objects.

designs.pre_registration_sketch:
Freeze before any scan read, in a committed file:
- code commit;
- per-object candidate IDs (generator, version, seed, frame list from crops.py);
- track-QA rule and the episodes it admits;
- router score definition: fs-style fidelity over all valid episodes, free-space violation, held-out silhouette IoU, area gate 0.5–2x lib, ambiguity gate (near-tied poses more than 5 mm apart), complexity penalty;
- incumbent per object (prim for pot, lid and rack; else library_v3) and the 10 % hysteresis;
- non-rigid stiffness grid {low, mid, high}, chosen GT-free.

Step 1: run the GT-free selection and commit selection.json.

Step 2: score every candidate exactly once with mesh_metric.py at the kit's eval seeds plus at least 6 random seeds (CD-O, ADD, flips); log the selector's Spearman per object.

Pass rule per object for the selected candidate:
- CD-O <= incumbent - 0.03 cm (lib, or prim where one passed);
- ADD <= incumbent + 1 mm;
- 0 flips over all seeds;
- any knob not chosen GT-free must pass at every grid value.

Spoon: no scan. Switch only if the GT-free score is at least 20 % better and there are 0 flips in 40 self-registration seeds.

Bookkeeping:
- no re-runs or edits after seeing scores; failures are logged;
- fixes become new candidates in a later round;
- every run's command, machine, commit and output path goes in docs/work-log.md;
- eval packing uses only passing public-built meshes, same frame as library_v3, with the owner's go-ahead.

scores.reasons:
Evidence is strong and honest. I re-tabulated the 48-candidate matrix and every [M] claim holds:
- the flat board plate on the sweeper wins standard fidelity, 3.274 vs library 4.216;
- the back-face-culled score prefers library over the cup lathe, 3.64 vs 3.745;
- 4 of 5 scores prefer library over the rack joint fit on ep26;
- untrimmed recall fails only on the dust pan, where the lid template's phantom strap scores rec3 0.716 vs 0.692.
The accuracy forecast (about 0.33 cm) is realistic. Swapping the already [D]-passing pot, lid and rack (0.367 [E]) plus the blue-cup ep21 lathe (0.523 -> 0.291, ADD 10.0 -> 4.1 mm, 0/7 flips [D], which already meets the pass-rule numbers) gives about 0.335 [E] with no new method. So this design's own CD-O value over existing work is close to zero on 17 of 36 pairs. Its upside is limited to the dust pan (2), sweeper (2), pitcher (1) and a cup handle increment.
The parts that would create new accuracy are unproven:
- Its primary selector, z-buffered depth-render agreement, is not built or tested.
- Its structure search, blind residual peeling with beam search over 10 parts, has no precedent here. [M] RANSAC explains only 24-60 % of points, and fitters on the wrong structure hit their bounds silently.
It keeps the hand-kit fallback for pot, lid and rack and has a real flip gate, so it is unlikely to lose pairs. It is also unlikely to win many new ones. Spoon (10) and beige cup (2) stay untouched.

judges.recommendation:
Neither design should be built in full yet. Under the accuracy lens, design 2's protocol scores slightly higher (5.5 vs 5). It keeps library-versus-new out of the GT-free selector, uses held-out episodes and has a beige-cup path. Its agent arm should be treated as optional.

Ordered by measured CD-O gain per pair:

1. Pre-register a $0 swap round for meshes that already meet the pass-rule numbers [D]:
   - pot 0.437 -> 0.342 (6 pairs);
   - lid 0.421 -> 0.220 (1 pair);
   - rack 0.555 -> 0.456 (5 pairs);
   - blue-cup lathe_obs ep21 0.523 -> 0.291, ADD 10.0 -> 4.1 mm, 0/7 flips, episode picked GT-free (5 pairs).

   About 0.335 cm overall [E] with no new method. That is roughly what either design forecasts.

2. Freeze a combined GT-free selector and test it on fresh candidates:
   - design 2's fs_frac gate (precision) AND design 1's untrimmed rec3 floor (completeness) are both required;
   - then rank by sum_mm;
   - plus design 1's bound-hit rejection, the 0-flip gate and a library-frame distance gate (< 5 mm median; [M] lid ep0 is 23-26 mm off).

   On the existing matrix this picks the reference on 8 of 8 objects [M, post hoc]. Each part alone fails: fs alone takes the sweeper plate and the pitcher fragment; recall alone takes the dust-pan phantom strap.

3. Build only design 2's router arm R, without the agent. Use design 1's generic part library, relative windows, unified marker detector and thickness grid that must pass at every value. Add existing [D]-scored GT-free variants as candidates: dust-pan Design O 0.251, sweeper single-episode TSDF 0.271. Prioritise by pairs:
   - cup handle sweep (5);
   - beige cup via v3x ep11 obs (2);
   - dust-pan plate set (2);
   - sweeper (2).

4. Spend the $10-20 agent Stage A only if R fails on an object.

5. The largest lever, the spoon (10 of 36 pairs, about 0.18 cm inferred), is untouched by both designs. It needs the owner-approved eval-depth job (about $2-4) and a decision on GT-free-only evidence. Re-tracking the beige cup and board 2 matters more than any mesher.

